<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!DOCTYPE mapper
  PUBLIC "-//mybatis.org//DTD Mapper 3.0//EN"
  "https://mybatis.org/dtd/mybatis-3-mapper.dtd">
<!--
  The first sliver of the study module, and it exists because Phase 2 needs it.

  Session lifecycle, queue serving and answer recording are Phase 3. BR-259 is not: it is an
  obligation of the *delete* path, which shipped in Phase 2 without it. What lives here is exactly
  the two lookups and the one write that rule names, and nothing else.
-->
<mapper namespace="com.memox.study.persistence.StudyMapper">

  <!--
    Open sessions a deletion invalidates (BR-259).

    **Two statements rather than one union, because the two reasons are genuinely different.** One
    is a session opened *on* a deck that is going away. The other is a session whose queue holds a
    card that is going away — and that second one is what catches a review of a whole root deck when
    a sub-deck three levels down is deleted: the session's `deck_id` is the root there, and the root
    is not in the batch. Drop the card-side lookup and that session stays `in_progress` over
    material the user can no longer see.

    Neither reads `cards` or `decks`, so neither needs a tombstone predicate: they take the id sets
    the deletion has already decided on, and they run BEFORE the marking so those ids still describe
    live rows.

    `status = 'in_progress'` and nothing else. A session that already completed, was abandoned or
    failed stays as it ended (BR-86) — a terminal end state is never overwritten, however much of
    its material is now gone.
  -->
  <select id="findOpenSessionIdsForDecks" resultType="string">
    SELECT id
      FROM study_sessions
     WHERE status = 'in_progress'
       AND deck_id IN
    <foreach collection="deckIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="deckId">#{deckId}</foreach>
  </select>

  <select id="findOpenSessionIdsForCards" resultType="string">
    SELECT DISTINCT q.session_id
      FROM study_queue_items q
      INNER JOIN study_sessions s ON s.id = q.session_id
     WHERE s.status = 'in_progress'
       AND q.card_id IN
    <foreach collection="cardIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="cardId">#{cardId}</foreach>
  </select>

  <!--
    The close-write, and the pair is written as one unit on purpose.

    BR-259: `status = invalidated` with `end_reason = content_deleted`, and the reason MUST be
    stored rather than inferred later. Nothing enforces which pairs are legal — the Postgres schema
    carries two independent CHECK lists, not a compound one — so the guarantee here is structural:
    this is the only statement that writes the pair, its caller cannot choose either half, and the
    values arrive as enum constants rather than literals.

    `ended_at` is a parameter, not `now()`. It is the same instant the batch carries as its
    `deleted_at`, because a session that ended a few milliseconds after the deletion it was
    invalidated for is a session nobody can line up with anything later.
  -->
  <update id="invalidateSessions">
    UPDATE study_sessions
       SET status = #{status,typeHandler=com.memox.study.persistence.StudySessionStatusTypeHandler}
         , end_reason = #{endReason,typeHandler=com.memox.study.persistence.StudySessionEndReasonTypeHandler}
         , ended_at = #{endedAt}
     WHERE id IN
    <foreach collection="sessionIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="sessionId">#{sessionId}</foreach>
       AND status = 'in_progress'
  </update>

</mapper>
